\title{Warm and with 8 workers the machine is at 1.0 to 1.5 times gruszka on the libraries and about twice its time on Mathlib}
\taxon{Insight}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{benchmark}
\tag{performance}
\tag{machine}
\meta{origin}{docs/BENCHMARKS.md}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Source: [rep-benchmarks](rep-benchmarks), section "Warm wall clock"; [the session on wave 6 and the pipeline](ses-CVTE). One process checks the same export again, every pass loads pipelined with checking, 8 workers on the 8 quiet performance cores of the aarch64 development host; warm is the median of the later passes. The baselines ran their arena run line with 8 threads in the arena container on the same cores, second of two runs. This is not the arena configuration.}
\table{
\tr{\th{Warm wall clock, s}\th{Init}\th{Std}\th{cedar}\th{reachability}\th{Mathlib}}
\tr{\td{machine, \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}, JVM launcher, first table}\td{0.96}\td{1.72}\td{2.21}\td{1.88}\td{25.6}}
\tr{\td{machine, \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}, native image, first table}\td{1.07}\td{1.88}\td{2.62}\td{2.66}\td{35.3}}
\tr{\td{reference engine, JVM launcher, first table}\td{1.70}\td{2.74}\td{3.62}\td{3.96}\td{68.58}}
\tr{\td{gruszka, first table}\td{1.01}\td{1.13}\td{2.07}\td{2.57}\td{12.5}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean, first table}\td{1.23}\td{1.79}\td{2.95}\td{0.96}\td{20.7}}
\tr{\td{machine, larger default set, native image, second table}\td{0.81}\td{1.51}\td{2.08}\td{0.61}\td{27.43}}
\tr{\td{machine, larger default set, JVM launcher, second table}\td{0.72}\td{2.92, best 1.43, not steady}\td{2.14, best 1.85, not steady}\td{0.56}\td{21.23}}
\tr{\td{gruszka, second table}\td{0.66}\td{1.06}\td{1.65}\td{2.36}\td{12.32}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean, second table}\td{1.04}\td{1.74}\td{2.69}\td{0.75}\td{18.52}}
}
\ul{
\li{First table, 17:37Z: the machine on the JVM is at 0.95, 1.52, 1.07, 0.73 and 2.05 times gruszka on Init, Std, cedar, reachability and Mathlib, and at 0.78, 0.96, 0.75, 1.96 and 1.24 times nano-lean.}
\li{Second table, 20:04Z to 20:30Z, with the switch set \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect,bodies,pchk,match,joint,code,casetrees,lazycases,directrules,lazyeval,same~} and a session cap of 1 MiB: the native image is at 1.23, 1.42, 1.26, 0.26 and 2.23 times gruszka; the JVM launcher at 1.72 times on Mathlib.}
\li{Against the reference engine the machine needs 0.47 to 0.63 times the wall clock below Mathlib and 0.37 times on Mathlib.}
\li{The pipeline turns 8 workers into a factor of 5.3 to 6.0 on Init, Std and cedar; gruszka gets 3.1 to 4.7 and nano-lean 4.1 to 4.8 from 8 threads. At one thread the machine on the JVM is level with nano-lean on those corpora and at 1.4 to 1.8 times gruszka.}
\li{In CPU time the distance is larger than in wall clock: 1.5, 1.8, 1.5 and 2.5 times gruszka in the first table.}
\li{The baselines do not get faster in a second run; their spread on this host is 5 to 15 percent, so differences below that are noise, ours included.}
}
\p{The second table is what the machine gave when selected by option in the afternoon of 2026-11-24. In that distribution it was the default engine for an hour and was taken back, see [making the machine the default engine](prob-W2FV). What separated it from gruszka on Mathlib is in [the gap to gruszka](prob-TJK3).}
\p{\strong{Later the same day} the default switch set gained eager reduction and the two proof-comparison shortcuts and the machine became the default engine. With that configuration the tables of the released jars, [the benchmark tables](rep-benchmarks), have Mathlib at 12.44 s on the JVM launcher and 15.46 s for the executable against 14.20 s for gruszka, 0.88 and 1.09 times; the executable is at 1.00, 0.95, 1.05 and 0.23 times gruszka on Init, Std, cedar and reachability. The title of this tree is the state of the two tables above.}
\p{See also: [the measurement method](design-measurement-method); [staging switches and the default switch set](design-switches).}
